<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Mozambique</h1><p class="st-meta">1975 CE – present · capital Maputo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A south-east African country, independent from Portugal in 1975, which went through a civil war from 1977 to 1992 and has held multiparty elections since 1994.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a> became independent on 25 June 1975, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frelimo">FRELIMO</a> had waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> campaign against Portuguese rule from September 1964 and Portugal's own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> fell in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> of April 1974. Most of the 250,000 Portuguese in Mozambique left within a year.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samora-machel">Samora Machel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frelimo">FRELIMO</a> built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> on Marxist principles with support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. From 1977 to 1992 it fought a civil war with the anti-communist RENAMO, backed by Rhodesia and later apartheid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. An estimated one million Mozambicans died, and both sides committed human rights violations. Machel died in a plane crash on 19 October 1986. Joaquim Chissano began reforms, a 1990 constitution provided for multiple parties, and the Rome General Peace Accords ended the war in October 1992.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Elections have been held since 1994, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frelimo">FRELIMO</a> winning each time; the results since 1999 have been disputed as fraudulent. Armando Guebuza served as president from 2005 and Filipe Nyusi from 15 January 2015. A low-intensity RENAMO insurgency ran from 2013 to 2019, and since 2017 Islamist groups have mounted an insurgency in the north. Daniel Chapo was sworn in as president on 15 January 2025.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mozambique.jpg" alt="Mozambique" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: FRELIMO · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mozambique.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1975 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Maputo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Samora Machel, Joaquim Chissano, Armando Guebuza, Filipe Nyusi, Daniel Chapo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (Catholic and Protestant), Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Mozambique</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=mozambique" aria-label="Mozambique on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Mozambique lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g030s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5198 4658L5198 4653"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5198 4653h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5198 4658h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5198 4658h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Maputo. <a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=mozambique">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
